<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ermengarde of Narbonne</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1127–1197</span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ermengarde was viscountess of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narbonne">Narbonne</a> from 1134 to 1192 and one of the most powerful women of 12th-century southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. She allied with the counts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toulouse">Toulouse</a>, took part in the siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tortosa">Tortosa</a> in 1148, sat as a judge and presided over a court praised by troubadours. She abdicated in favour of her nephew Pedro de Lara.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ermengarde was the daughter of Aimery II of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narbonne">Narbonne</a> and his first wife, also named Ermengarde. Her father was killed at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-fraga">Battle of Fraga</a> on 17 July 1134, fighting the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almoravid-empire">Almoravids</a> alongside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-i-of-aragon">Alfonso I of Aragon</a>, and his only known son had died before him, around 1130. Aimery left two underage daughters, Ermengarde and her half-sister Ermessinde, so the viscounty passed to Ermengarde at about five years old. Narbonne, close to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> on the Aude River, held a strategic place in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/languedoc">Languedoc</a> and was coveted by the counts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toulouse">Toulouse</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-trencavel">Trencavel</a> viscounts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carcassonne">Carcassonne</a> and the lords of Montpellier.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1142 Alfonso Jordan, Count of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toulouse">Toulouse</a>, tried to marry the adolescent Ermengarde, which would have brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narbonne">Narbonne</a> under Toulouse's direct control. A coalition of Occitan lords led by Roger II of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beziers">Béziers</a>, viscount of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carcassonne">Carcassonne</a>, Béziers, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albi">Albi</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/razes">Razès</a>, opposed him and married her to Roger's vassal Bernard IV of Anduze. Alfonso was defeated and captured, and was released only after making peace and restoring Ermengarde and her husband to the viscounty.</p><p>Ermengarde was known for her political and legal skill and made alliances with powerful men, among them King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-vii-of-france">Louis VII of France</a>, who endorsed her rule when the people of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narbonne">Narbonne</a> began to question it. The historian Fredric Cheyette argues that she did not exploit her city's trade but ensured for decades that its merchants were cared for. In 1177 she joined Gui Guerrejat, Bernard Ato V of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nimes">Nîmes</a> and Agde, William VIII of Montpellier and Gui Burgundion in an alliance against Raymond VI of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toulouse">Toulouse</a>, whose power had grown when he became ruler of Melgueil.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Her fame reached northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>: around 1190 André le Chapelain's Treatise on Courtly Love attributed five of its twenty-one judgements of love to her, probably fictional ones, beside three of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eleanor-of-aquitaine">Eleanor of Aquitaine</a> and seven of her daughter Marie. She corresponded with the troubadours Peire Rogier, Giraut de Bornelh and Peire d'Alvergne and the trobairitz Azalais de Porcairagues, and she is believed to have welcomed Rognvald II of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orkney">Orkney</a>, who composed skaldic poetry for her. Childless, she made Pedro Manrique de Lara, eldest surviving son of Ermessinde, her heir, abdicated in his favour in 1192 and retired to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/perpignan">Perpignan</a>, where she died on 14 October 1197.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ermengarde of Narbonne” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Viscountess Ermengard</b>, who ruled from 1134 to 1192, presided over a court celebrated by troubadours and steered between Toulouse and Barcelona.</blockquote><a href="/state/viscounty-of-narbonne">Viscounty of Narbonne</a></li></ul></section>
